1 OCCU Generalprokurør (Generalprokurøren var under Enevælden Kancelliets ledende sekretær), Estatsråd
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Peder Lauridsen Scavenius==
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Family:==
2 CONT
2 CONT *Parents: Compasses in Roskilde, later bishop of Kbh. Laurids Mortensen S. (1589-1655) and Else Pedersdatter (1601-53). 
2 CONT *Married 16.9.1658 in Kbh. with Sister Jacobsdatter, born 22.7.1639 in Kbh. (Ms.), died 24.6.1693 sd., D. Of the chief priest, later bishop of Aarhus Jacob Matthiesen (1602-60) and Anna Bartholin (1613-82).
2 CONT
2 CONT The General Procurator was during the unanimity cancelli's leading secretary. He also had the influential position to present the case of the cancelli to the king of the Konseillet.
2 CONT
2 CONT Among the institutions that the monarchy's introduction in Denmark-Norway called for alive was also a general commissioner. This was originally sorted under the Chambers of Commerce, and the General Procurator's duties were also i
2 CONC nitially financial in nature, as he had to ensure that the state's claims were recovered. In the long run, however, he was exaggerated to guard against the fact that nothing happened to the detriment of sovereignty and the soverei
2 CONC gnty of the king or to the weakness of the royal interests, for which purpose he had to keep diligent correspondence with the officials in the provinces and also regularly to take care of them. Of this, during the Christian 5th, a
2 CONC  comprehensive company with reports and reports was developed so that the office became one of the most important in the state.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1730, it became more closely linked to the chancellor, in which general prosecutor was given a seat, and in the following time, its significance increased even further, as its holder gradually became the crown's legal consultan
2 CONC t and counselor in all doubtful court cases. He listened to the preparation of new and more important laws and devices; it was his duty and duty to draw attention to deficiencies or discrepancies in the existing legislation that c
2 CONC ould change, and he was obliged, after special order, to seat in the committees that were reduced for various purposes just like him when In addition, it was possible to order travel to inspect the various sorting devices througho
2 CONC ut the county and report on their condition, accompanied by proposals for improvements and extensions.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thus, it was an important and difficult task and, as long as it existed, the office has only been entrusted to the best Danish and Norwegian lawyers: Peder Lauridsen Scavenius (1660-85) and others. See Wikipeida.,
2 CONT
