1 OCCU Borgmester i Helsingør (1536), Øresundstolder 1548.
1 TITL Borgmester
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.carlin.no/slekt/Ancestors/ff/ana39548.html
2 CONT (No parents)
2 CONT -----
2 CONT https://forum.rotter.se/index.php?topic=55097.msg801957#msg801957
2 CONT -----
2 CONT Född i Skottland av adlig släkt. 1536 borgmästare i Helsingör. 1548 Öresundstullare. Den rikaste personen i Helsingör och ägde gårdarna på Stengade 70, 72 o 74. En gård i Köpenhamn och en i Borsholm. God kontakt med kung Kristian 
2 CONC III, som var fadder till en av hans söner. Gjorde inköp till hovet. Skrev brev till kung James VI i Skottland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * LejeI, Sander (LeylIe, Leyl, LeyelI), Raadmand, Borgmester 1536, Tolder, död 21/2 1560.
2 CONT * G. m. Eline Davidsdalter. E. e .• Jens Henniksen. Faderen: D. Thomessøn, Borgmester Nr. 11.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Børn: Frederik, f. 1536, Borgmester Nr. 21.
2 CONT * Hans, Cannik i Roskilde, död 31/5 1587.
2 CONT * David, Raadmand Nr. 46.
2 CONT * Dorthe, g. m. Hans Olsen, Raadmand og Borgmester i Kjøbenhavn.
2 CONT * Datter, död 18/11 1592, ugift.
2 CONT =-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-==-
2 CONT
2 CONT Rune Eriksson:
2 CONT  I am researching the family history Leijel in Denmark. The family came from Scotland sometime during the first half of the 1500s. The first Leijel was then called Alexander Lyle and descended from the clan Lyle (Lord Lyle). Alexa
2 CONC nder changed his name to Sander Leijel. I'm looking for his ancestors in Scotland and lacks the "missing link" between the Scottish and Danish branch. Immigrants had at that time brought a change of address issued by, as a rule, t
2 CONC he urban, immigrant who came from the mayor. (I have found another Leijel from Scotland, who immigrated to Sweden around the turn of 1500 and 1600s and his change of address I found on the Royal Library's manuscript archives in St
2 CONC ockholm). Is there something similar archive of documents from the 1500s in Denmark or can it be that someone else on Anbytarforum researching family Lyle (Leijel) and found the link between Sander Leijel and the Scottish lord Lyl
2 CONC e clan?
2 CONT - - - - - - - - - Have you researched about the family Lyle (Leyell, Leijell) mentioned in the Scottish city of Dundee in sennmedeltid and 1500s? The Scottish family Wedderburn came from this town and settled down also in Elsinore
2 CONC , where the name was changed to Wederborn and then when they moved to Sweden to Wederborg. My thought is that it might have been one and the same moving chain of castles networks that come to Elsinore. The need for timber and timb
2 CONC er products to Scotland was huge in the 1500s after hundreds of years of harvesting as förrött forests. Via Elsinore imported timber from Sweden (genera Wederborg (aka Wedderburn) came through this wood trade to eventually establi
2 CONC sh themselves in the city of Växjö (Småland)) in the early 1600s.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://aforum.genealogi.se/discus/messages/241/342304.html?1369674266
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Alexander Lyall served as Mayor of Elsinore for 23 years. He started a dynasty in the town who mostly worked in similar positions. On 27 August 1542 Hans Murdesoun made a supplication regarding David Carnegie, servant to Sanders L
2 CONC eil, 'Scotsman in Elsinore', and master of one of his ships against a Zealand skipper. Lyall was later appointed director of the Sound Toll in Elsinore in 1548 and was responsible for much of the reorganisation of it. The Len, or 
2 CONC small administrative district of Ydernaes was subdivided into farms. Four of these were granted to Sander Lyall up until 1566, the Toll Officer from Elsinore. A further 9 were added in Norre Bedstrup in August 1550. ..............
2 CONC ................................ See Acts of the Lords of Council in Public Affaires, 1501-1554, edited by R. Kerrhanny (Edinburgh, 1932), p.521, 27 August 1542, Supplication by Hans Murdesoun; K. R. Erslev Danmarks Len of Lensmae
2 CONC nd 1513-1596 (Christiania, 1879), p.95; C. Smout, 'The European Lifeline' in G. Menzies (ed.), In Search of Scotland (Edinburgh, 2001), p.121; L. Baillie (ed.), Scandinavian Biographical Index (4 vols., London Melbourne, Munich Ne
2 CONC w Jersey , n.d.), p.1233; Steve Murdoch, Network North: Scottish Kin, Commercial and Covert Associations in Northern Europe, 1603-1746 (Brill, Leiden, 2006), p.213. 
2 CONT
2 CONT www.st-andrews.ac.uk/history/ssne/index.php
2 CONT
2 CONT Elsinore in the 16th century (Helsingør, Denmark)
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Tayside ports there were two main routes to the Baltic (as described in the 15th century Hanseatic Segelhandbuch): the Belt route following the east coast of Jutland through the Great Belt or, alternatively, the Sound, al
2 CONC ong the west coast of what is now Sweden, through the Sound by Elsinore. The Belt route made navigational sense for voyages to Rostock and other Western ports, the Sound for Danzig and the East. Another reason for the popularity o
2 CONC f the Belt route in the early sixteenth century was that, although custom tolls were to be paid on both routes, this was far easier to avoid on going through the Belt. The placing by the Danes of a Man-of-War in the Belt in 1555 c
2 CONC hanged this and the time lost in awaiting clearance at Elsinore was balanced by the market information which could be gleaned there. The Scottish influence in the town was already considerable by the 16th century. The Scottish mer
2 CONC chant, Alexander Lyall, was borgmester (mayor) twice and in charge of the Sound Toll. A memorial was erected to him in Sankt Olai's church and his house at 70 Stengade still stands. His influence extended to being adviser to the K
2 CONC ing and godfather to one of his sons. Descendants of his were also mayors of the town and one was governor of Denmark's colony in India, Trankebar.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.dmcsoft.com/tamh/port.php?port=Elsinore&century=
2 CONT
