1 NAME Frederik Sandersen /Lejel/ Borgmester, Tolder
2 GIVN Frederik Sandersen
2 SURN Lejel
2 NSFX Borgmester, Tolder
2 _MARNM Leiel
2 NICK LejeI
2 NICK LeylIe
2 NICK Leyl
2 NICK Leijel
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1536
2 PLAC Helsingør
2 ADDR
3 CTRY Danmark
1 DEAT
2 DATE 13 MAR 1601
2 PLAC Helsingør
2 ADDR
3 CTRY Danmark
1 BURI
2 DATE 16 MAR 1601
1 OCCU Borgmester og Tolder i Helsingør, Kunglig tullare, borgmästare Helsingör, Rådmand i Helsingør 1566-79, toldskriver smst. 1579, Øresundstolder 1583, borgmester 1591-1601.
1 TITL Borgmästare, och rådmann
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} sources:
2 CONT http://www.carlin.no/slekt/Ancestors/ff/ana19774.html
2 CONT -----
2 CONT Frederik Lyall (c.1536-1601) was the son of Alexander Lyall. He married Pernille Olesdatter, daughter of the mayor of Copenhagen Oluf Skriver and consort Ingeborg Mortensdatter. She died by by 12/2/1565 and he married his second w
2 CONC ife, Kirstine Jensdatter (children: Alexander, Ingeborg, plus ten other children who died from the plague 1581). Lyall's third wife, Sophie Hansdatter, survived him and who with three stepchildren had an epitaph set up in St Olai 
2 CONC to his memory. The family had two pews in St Olai. Lyall became a burgess by 12/1/1562 and is known to have paid taxes between 1561-6 and 1581-3. Like others in his family he became a customs official (toldskriver) by 14/3/1564 an
2 CONC d one of the wardens of the Hospital by 1564. Lyall also became a Councillor between 1566-1579, and 'leading director' of the Sound Toll on 28/4/1583. Added to his CV was his appointment as mayor of Elsinore on 17/7/1591. General 
2 CONC Peter Young intended to stop at Lyall's house on his way back from Germany in 1598. Lyall maintained his links to Scotland and recommended Hans Eler of Danzig to William Stewart in order that his business in Scotland might be supp
2 CONC orted by James VI 7/12/1593. His other business connections included his appointment as factor for George Bruce of Culross on 10/11/1589. Another Scottish connection can be shown through his claim of 400 daler on David Wedderburn'
2 CONC s estate on 11/6/1594. Lyall also had business interests in Denmark and by easter 1600, he had lent 800 daler at 6% to the town of Malmo. 
2 CONT
2 CONT .............................................................. 
2 CONT
2 CONT T. Riis, Should Auld Acquaintance Be Forgot (Odense, 1988), II, pp.231-2; LAS. Helsingor Radstue no.449, [1573] and 5/7/1573; MStA. Magistratens arkiv, BRR 1591, p.35; ibid. Borgerskapets i Malmo arkiv G Ia:3: Kamnarsrakenskap 160
2 CONC 0; NLS. Adv. 33.1.11. Denmilne 28, fol.21r.-22v., cf. 29r.-30v.; Danish Rigsarkiv Kanc. B 46 no. 146/1; Danmarks kirker, Frederiksborg Amt I, p.177-9 fig. 94 i, 181 no.26, 237-8 fig. 124-5, 528; Extracts from the Records of the Bu
2 CONC rgh of Edinburgh A.D. 1589-1603, p.9; Steve Murdoch, Network North: Scottish Kin, Commercial and Covert Associations in Northern Europe, 1603-1746 (Brill, Leiden, 2006), p.152. 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.st-andrews.ac.uk/history/ssne/item.php?id=1141&id2=1141
2 CONT
